# Page counts

Page counts tell you how many times each page of the app was opened each day,
added up across every site on your instance. Use them to see which reports and
features people use, and which they do not.

<Callout type="warning" title="Off by default. Update your privacy policy first.">

  Page counts are off on every new install. If your privacy policy says that
  your sites have no analytics, change it before you turn this on, and give your
  users the notice that your policy requires. There is suggested wording at the
  end of this page.

</Callout>

## What is stored

One row for each page and day, in the `page_view_counts` table of your own
database:

| Column | Example | What it is |
|--------|---------|------------|
| `date` | `2026-11-27` | The day of the views, in UTC. |
| `route_id` | `/(authenticated)/reports/agp` | Which page of the app was opened. This is the page's name in the app's code, not its address. |
| `count` | `140` | How many times that page was opened that day, on all sites together. |

That is all. The rows stay in your database. Nocturne does not send them to the
Nocturne project or to any other service.

## What is not stored

- **No person.** No user, member, account, session or cookie.
- **No site.** No tenant, slug or domain. The counts of all sites are added
  together.
- **No device.** No IP address, user agent, browser, screen size or language.
- **No address details.** No query string and no values from the address, such
  as report dates, record IDs or share tokens. Only the page's name.
- **No time of day.** Only the date.
- **No health data.** No glucose, treatment or other health values.

The web app sends the page name to the API to count it. Like any other request,
that request can appear in your server's ordinary request logs and traces. The
page-count feature does not add anything to them.

## How a view is counted

- The app counts a view when a page opens in the browser, including the first
  page of a visit.
- A change that stays on the same page, such as a new date range on a report,
  is not a new view.
- The API accepts only the names of pages that exist in the app. It refuses all
  other names.
- Each address can send at most 120 views a minute. This stops one script from
  filling the counts.
- The API keeps the counts in memory and writes them to the database every five
  minutes and when it stops. Today's counts can be up to five minutes behind.

## Turn it on

Set the value to `true`, then restart the stack. The API and the web app read
the same setting, so the web app sends nothing while the API would not count
it.

| Install method | Setting |
|----------------|---------|
| [Install script](/docs/installation/install-script) or [Docker Compose](/docs/installation/docker-compose) | `PAGE_COUNTS_ENABLED=true` in the `.env` file |
| [Portainer](/docs/installation/portainer) | **Count page views**: `true` |
| Helm | `telemetry.pageCounts.enabled: true` |
| Your own containers | `Telemetry__PageCounts__Enabled=true` on the API, and `PUBLIC_PAGE_COUNTS_ENABLED=true` on the web app |

To turn it off, set the value back to `false` and restart. The app then sends
nothing and the API counts nothing.

## Read the counts

Ask the API as a platform administrator, or with the instance key:

```bash
curl "https://example.com/api/v4/admin/telemetry/page-counts?from=2026-11-01&to=2026-11-27" \
  -H "X-Instance-Key: $(printf '%s' "$INSTANCE_KEY" | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)" \
  -H "X-Instance-Service: page-counts"
```

`from` and `to` are UTC dates and include both days. Without them you get the
last 30 days. The answer is a list:

```json
[
  { "date": "2026-11-27", "routeId": "/(authenticated)/reports/agp", "count": 140 },
  { "date": "2026-11-27", "routeId": "/(authenticated)/calendar", "count": 52 }
]
```

### Find pages nobody opened

The list above holds only pages that were opened. To see the pages with no
views, get the list of every page the app counts, and remove the pages that are
in the counts:

```bash
curl "https://example.com/api/v4/admin/telemetry/page-routes" \
  -H "X-Instance-Key: $(printf '%s' "$INSTANCE_KEY" | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)" \
  -H "X-Instance-Service: page-counts"
```

The answer is a list of page names in alphabetical order. It does not change
between days, and it is the same while page counts are off:

```json
[
  "/(authenticated)",
  "/(authenticated)/alerts",
  "/(authenticated)/reports/agp"
]
```

## How long counts are kept

The API deletes counts older than 400 days, once a day. It does this while page
counts are off too, so old counts still go away after you turn the feature off.
To delete all counts now, run `DELETE FROM page_view_counts;` on your database.

## Suggested privacy policy wording

Change this to match your service, and have it checked by someone qualified
for your location:

> **Page usage counts.** We count how many times each page of the app is opened
> each day, added up across all the sites we host. We store only the name of
> the page (for example, "AGP report"), the date and the total. We do not store
> who opened the page, which site it was on, your IP address, your device or
> browser, or any of your health data. We use these totals to learn which
> features people use, so that we know where to put our effort. We keep them
> for 400 days and then delete them.
